1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,262
Chapter 22: Normal Labor.

3
00:00:05,262 --> 00:00:26,087
Fetal Orientation Mechanisms Of Labor Normal Labor Characteristics Management Of Normal Labor Management Of First-Stage Labor Management Of Second-Stage Labor Labor Management Protocols References Labor is the process that leads to childbirth. It begins with the onset of regular uterine contractions and ends with delivery of the newborn and expulsion of the placenta.

4
00:00:26,087 --> 00:00:29,825
Pregnancy and birth are physiological processes.

5
00:00:29,825 --> 00:00:34,450
Thus, labor and delivery should be considered normal for most women.

6
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:36,625
Fetal Orientation.

7
00:00:36,625 --> 00:00:38,912
# Fetal Lie.

8
00:00:38,912 --> 00:00:44,762
Fetal position within the birth canal is critical to labor progress and to the delivery route.

9
00:00:44,762 --> 00:00:51,000
It should be determined in early labor, and sonography can be implemented for unclear cases.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,062
Important relationships include fetal lie, presentation, attitude, and position.

11
00:00:57,062 --> 00:01:03,225
Of these, fetal lie describes the relationship of the fetal long axis to that of the mother.

12
00:01:03,225 --> 00:01:08,637
In more than 99 percent of labors at term, the fetal lie is longitudinal.

13
00:01:08,637 --> 00:01:11,537
A transverse lie is less frequent.

14
00:01:11,537 --> 00:01:18,550
Occasionally, the fetal and maternal axes may cross at a 45-degree angle to form an oblique lie.

15
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:23,587
This is unstable and becomes longitudinal or transverse during labor.

16
00:01:23,587 --> 00:01:26,287
# Fetal Presentation.

17
00:01:26,287 --> 00:01:32,725
The presenting part is the portion of the fetal body either within or in closest proximity to the birth canal.

18
00:01:32,725 --> 00:01:37,137
It usually can be felt through the cervix on vaginal examination.

19
00:01:37,137 --> 00:01:46,125
In longitudinal lies, the presenting part is either the fetal head or the breech, creating cephalic and breech presentations, respectively.

20
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:52,350
When the fetus lies with the long axis transversely, the shoulder is considered the presenting part.

21
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:59,125
Cephalic presentations are subclassified according to the relationship between the head and body of the fetus .

22
00:01:59,125 --> 00:02:04,487
Ordinarily, the head is flexed sharply so that the chin contacts the thorax.

23
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:12,350
The occipital fontanel is the presenting part, and this presentation is referred to as a vertex or occiput presentation.

24
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:22,812
Much less often, the fetal neck may be sharply extended so that the occiput and back come into contact, and the face is foremost in the birth canal—face presentation.

25
00:02:22,812 --> 00:02:26,950
The fetal head may assume a position between these extremes.

26
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:34,225
When the neck is only partly flexed, the anterior (large) fontanel may present—sinciput presentation.

27
00:02:34,225 --> 00:02:39,900
When the neck is only partially extended, the brow may emerge—brow presentation.

28
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:43,087
These latter two are usually transient.

29
00:02:43,087 --> 00:02:53,437
As labor progresses, sinciput and brow presentations almost always convert into occiput or face presentations by neck flexion or extension, respectively.

30
00:02:53,437 --> 00:02:56,287
If not, dystocia can develop .

31
00:02:56,287 --> 00:02:58,850
Figure 22-1.

32
00:02:58,850 --> 00:03:02,437
Longitudinal lie, cephalic presentation.

33
00:03:02,437 --> 00:03:11,850
Differences in attitude of the fetal body in (A) occiput, (B) sinciput, (C) brow, and (D) face presentations.

34
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:16,812
Note changes in fetal attitude as the fetal head becomes less flexed.

35
00:03:16,812 --> 00:03:21,162
The fetus at term usually presents occiput rather than breech.

36
00:03:21,162 --> 00:03:32,275
Although the fetal head at term is slightly larger than the breech, the entire podalic pole of the fetus—that is, the breech and extremities—is bulkier and more mobile than the cephalic pole.

37
00:03:32,275 --> 00:03:36,075
The cephalic pole is composed of the fetal head only.

38
00:03:36,075 --> 00:03:43,975
Logically, the fetus orients its polarity to make use of the roomier fundus for its bulkier and more mobile podalic pole.

39
00:03:43,975 --> 00:03:54,875
The breech presentation rate drops with gestational age, but 2 to 3 percent of singletons are breech at delivery (Bin, 2016; Toijonen, 2020).

40
00:03:54,875 --> 00:04:00,887
The three general breech configurations are frank, complete, and footling presentations.

41
00:04:00,887 --> 00:04:09,162
Management of the breech presenting fetus and of those with transverse lie is found in Chapters 28 and 23, respectively.

42
00:04:09,162 --> 00:04:11,662
# Fetal Attitude.

43
00:04:11,662 --> 00:04:19,012
In the later months of pregnancy, the fetus assumes a characteristic posture described as attitude or habitus.

44
00:04:19,012 --> 00:04:27,850
As a rule, the fetus forms an ovoid mass that corresponds roughly to the shape of the uterine cavity (see Fig. 22-1A).

45
00:04:27,850 --> 00:04:32,400
The fetus becomes folded upon itself to create a convex back.

46
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:40,650
The head is sharply flexed, the chin nearly contacts with the chest, the thighs are flexed over the abdomen, and the legs bend at the knee.

47
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:43,200
In cephalic presentations,

48
00:04:43,348 --> 00:04:48,048
the arms usually lie across the thorax or parallel to the sides.

49
00:04:47,998 --> 00:04:52,198
The umbilical cord fills the space between the extremities.

50
00:04:52,198 --> 00:04:57,323
This posture results from fetal growth and accommodation to the uterine cavity.

51
00:04:57,323 --> 00:05:04,223
Exceptions form as the fetal head becomes progressively more extended from occiput to face presentations.

52
00:05:04,223 --> 00:05:13,360
This results in a progressive change in fetal attitude from a convex (flexed) to a concave (extended) contour of the vertebral column.

53
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:20,160
This progression is seen in Figure 22-1, panels B through D. # Fetal Position.

54
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:27,898
Position refers to the relationship of a defined portion of the fetal presenting part to either the right or left side of the birth canal.

55
00:05:27,898 --> 00:05:36,948
The fetal occiput, chin (mentum), and sacrum are the defined landmarks in occiput, face, and breech presentations, respectively.

56
00:05:36,948 --> 00:05:51,573
Because the defined presenting part may be on either the mother’s left or right side, possible designations are left and right occipital (LO and RO), left and right mental (LM and RM), and left and right sacral (LS and RS).

57
00:05:51,573 --> 00:06:02,010
Further, the relationship of the defined landmark to the anterior (A), transverse (T), or posterior (P) portion of the maternal pelvis is considered .

58
00:06:02,010 --> 00:06:09,610
Thus, in an occiput presentation, the presentation and position may be abbreviated in clockwise fashion as shown.

59
00:06:09,610 --> 00:06:12,098
Figure 22-2.

60
00:06:12,098 --> 00:06:15,673
Longitudinal lie, occiput presentation.

61
00:06:15,673 --> 00:06:19,860
A. Left occiput anterior (LOA) position.

62
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:24,173
B. Left occiput posterior (LOP) position.

63
00:06:24,173 --> 00:06:28,610
C. Right occiput transverse (ROT) position.

64
00:06:28,610 --> 00:06:38,985
In shoulder presentations, the acromion process of the scapula is the portion of the fetus arbitrarily chosen for its orientation to the mother’s right or left side.

65
00:06:38,985 --> 00:06:46,285
In addition, the back may be directed anteriorly or posteriorly and also superiorly or inferiorly.

66
00:06:46,285 --> 00:06:57,098
Thus, it is customary to further distinguish right or left varieties as dorsoanterior and dorsoposterior (Fig. 23-7, p. 442).

67
00:06:57,098 --> 00:07:06,135
Another term used is transverse lie, with back up or back down, which is clinically important when deciding incision type for cesarean delivery.

68
00:07:06,135 --> 00:07:08,535
# Diagnosis.

69
00:07:08,535 --> 00:07:17,023
To diagnose fetal presentation and position, Leopold maneuvers, vaginal examination, and sonography are primary tools.

70
00:07:17,023 --> 00:07:25,560
First, abdominal examination can be conducted systematically employing the four maneuvers described by Leopold in 1894 .

71
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:30,335
The mother lies supine and comfortably positioned with her abdomen bared.

72
00:07:30,335 --> 00:07:37,560
Leopold maneuvers can be performed throughout the latter months of pregnancy and during and between the contractions of labor.

73
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:48,485
These may be difficult if not impossible to perform and interpret if the patient is obese, if amnionic fluid volume is excessive, or if the placenta is anteriorly implanted.

74
00:07:48,485 --> 00:07:51,098
Figure 22-3.

75
00:07:51,098 --> 00:07:59,485
Leopold maneuvers (A-D) performed in fetus with a longitudinal lie and left occiput anterior (LOA) position.

76
00:07:59,485 --> 00:08:02,948
The first maneuver assesses the uterine fundus.

77
00:08:02,948 --> 00:08:07,060
It permits identification of fetal lie and presentation.

78
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:12,610
The breech feels large and nodular, whereas the head is large, hard, and round.

79
00:08:12,610 --> 00:08:16,923
The second maneuver can help define the presenting part’s position.

80
00:08:16,923 --> 00:08:22,973
Palms are placed on either side of the maternal abdomen, and gentle but deep pressure is exerted.

81
00:08:22,973 --> 00:08:27,623
On one side, a hard, resistant structure is felt—the back.

82
00:08:27,623 --> 00:08:33,785
On the other, numerous small, irregular, mobile parts are felt—the fetal extremities.

83
00:08:33,785 --> 00:08:36,960
These may move in response to palpation.

84
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:40,960
The back indicates the side on which the occiput lies.

85
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:45,510
In occiput anterior positions, the convex back is felt.

86
00:08:45,510 --> 00:08:51,073
In occiput posterior positions, nodular extremity parts are appreciated.

87
00:08:51,073 --> 00:08:55,260
The third maneuver aids confirmation of fetal presentation.

88
00:08:55,260 --> 00:09:02,198
The thumb and fingers of one hand grasp the lower portion of the maternal abdomen just above the symphysis pubis.

89
00:09:02,198 --> 00:09:08,010
If the presenting part is not engaged, a movable mass will be felt, usually the head.

90
00:09:08,010 --> 00:09:12,735
The differentiation between head and breech is made as in the first maneuver.

91
00:09:12,735 --> 00:09:16,498
The fourth maneuver helps determine the degree of descent.

92
00:09:16,498 --> 00:09:23,985
The examiner faces the mother’s feet, and the fingertips of both hands are positioned on either side of the presenting part.

93
00:09:23,985 --> 00:09:29,660
They exert inward pressure and then slide caudad along the axis of the pelvic inlet.

94
00:09:29,660 --> 00:09:30,998
In

95
00:09:31,156 --> 00:09:41,306
many instances, when the head has descended into the pelvis, the anterior shoulder or the space created by the neck may be differentiated readily from the hard head.

96
00:09:41,256 --> 00:10:00,281
At least in the past, experienced clinicians have accurately identified fetal malpresentation using Leopold maneuvers with a high sensitivity—88 percent, specificity—94 percent, positive predictive value—74 percent, and negative predictive value—97 percent .

97
00:10:00,281 --> 00:10:05,868
With experience, it is possible to estimate the size of the fetus with these maneuvers .

98
00:10:05,868 --> 00:10:17,106
However, and especially with an obese woman, estimates by palpation and actual birth weights often correlate poorly (Fox, 2009; Preyer, 2019).

99
00:10:17,106 --> 00:10:21,106
Vaginal examination complements Leopold maneuvers.

100
00:10:21,106 --> 00:10:33,056
Before labor, the diagnosis of fetal presentation and position by vaginal examination is challenging because the presenting part must be palpated through a closed cervix and lower uterine segment.

101
00:10:33,056 --> 00:10:46,468
However, with labor and cervical dilation, cephalic presentations and their positions are recognized by palpation of the various fetal sutures and fontanels (Fig. 29-1, p. 534).

102
00:10:46,468 --> 00:10:55,993
Palpation of facial features signals a face presentation, whereas digital delineation of the fetal sacrum and perineum suggests breech presentation.

103
00:10:55,993 --> 00:11:01,681
With a transverse lie, ribs, scapula, or clavicle may be identified.

104
00:11:01,681 --> 00:11:07,218
Sonography is used to confirm suspected abnormal fetal presentation or lie.

105
00:11:07,218 --> 00:11:11,206
Sonography can also help clarify fetal position.

106
00:11:11,206 --> 00:11:19,506
For the occiput presenting fetus, a transducer is placed transversely on the lower maternal abdomen and moved toward the symphysis.

107
00:11:19,506 --> 00:11:27,543
In a fetus positioned occiput anterior, its spine is identified anteriorly and followed to its union with its occiput.

108
00:11:27,543 --> 00:11:34,031
The angle between the two is wide and increases as the head flexes and descends into the maternal pelvis.

109
00:11:34,031 --> 00:11:39,993
In occiput posterior positions, fetal orbits and nasal bridge lie anteriorly .

110
00:11:39,993 --> 00:11:52,381
Compared with digital interrogation, sonography for fetal head position determination during second-stage labor is more accurate (Ramphul, 2014; Wiafe, 2016).

111
00:11:52,381 --> 00:11:54,581
Mechanisms Of Labor.

112
00:11:54,581 --> 00:12:00,481
Positional changes of the presenting part are needed for the fetus to navigate through the pelvic canal.

113
00:12:00,481 --> 00:12:11,556
Called the mechanisms of labor or cardinal movements of labor, these are engagement, descent, flexion, internal rotation, extension, external rotation, and expulsion.

114
00:12:11,556 --> 00:12:15,843
During labor, these movements show great temporal overlap.

115
00:12:15,843 --> 00:12:21,031
For example, as part of engagement, the head both flexes and descends .

116
00:12:21,031 --> 00:12:30,381
Concurrently, uterine contractions effect important modifications in fetal attitude, especially after the head has descended into the pelvis.

117
00:12:30,381 --> 00:12:38,918
These changes consist mainly of fetal straightening, loss of dorsal convexity, and closer application of the extremities to the body.

118
00:12:38,918 --> 00:12:47,881
As a result, the fetal ovoid is transformed into a cylinder, with the smallest possible cross section typically passing through the birth canal.

119
00:12:47,881 --> 00:12:50,493
Figure 22-4.

120
00:12:50,493 --> 00:12:55,893
Cardinal movements of labor and delivery from a left occiput anterior position.

121
00:12:55,893 --> 00:12:58,156
# Engagement.

122
00:12:58,156 --> 00:13:05,406
In an occiput presentation, passage of the biparietal diameter through the pelvic inlet defines engagement.

123
00:13:05,406 --> 00:13:11,768
The fetal head may engage during the last few weeks of pregnancy or not until after labor commences.

124
00:13:11,768 --> 00:13:21,018
In many multiparas and some nulliparas, the fetal head is freely movable above the pelvic inlet at labor onset and is often referred to as “floating.

125
00:13:21,018 --> 00:13:32,793
In one study of 5341 nulliparas, lack of fetal head engagement before labor onset did not affect vaginal delivery rates in either spontaneous or induced labor .

126
00:13:32,793 --> 00:13:40,043
In most cases, the vertex enters the pelvis with the sagittal suture lying in the transverse pelvic diameter.

127
00:13:40,043 --> 00:13:48,131
Left occiput transverse (LOT) position is slightly more common than right occiput transverse (ROT) position .

128
00:13:48,131 --> 00:13:55,143
However, the sagittal suture may not lie exactly midway between the symphysis and the sacral promontory.

129
00:13:55,143 --> 00:14:03,681
The sagittal suture frequently is deflected off the midline, either posteriorly toward the promontory or anteriorly toward the symphysis .

130
00:14:03,681 --> 00:14:10,368
Such lateral deflection to a more anterior or posterior position in the pelvis is called asynclitism.

131
00:14:10,368 --> 00:14:12,568
If the sagittal suture

132
00:14:12,724 --> 00:14:23,111
approaches the sacral promontory, more of the anterior parietal bone presents itself to the examining fingers, and the condition is called anterior asynclitism.

133
00:14:23,061 --> 00:14:33,736
If, however, the sagittal suture lies close to the symphysis, more of the posterior parietal bone will present, and the condition is called posterior asynclitism.

134
00:14:33,736 --> 00:14:37,711
With extreme asynclitism, an ear may be palpable.

135
00:14:37,711 --> 00:14:40,399
Figure 22-5.

136
00:14:40,399 --> 00:14:43,211
Synclitism and asynclitism.

137
00:14:43,211 --> 00:14:47,549
Moderate degrees of asynclitism are the rule in normal labor.

138
00:14:47,549 --> 00:14:53,536
Successive fetal head shifting from posterior to anterior asynclitism aids descent.

139
00:14:53,536 --> 00:15:01,961
However, if severe, the condition is a common reason for cephalopelvic disproportion even with an otherwise normal-sized pelvis.

140
00:15:01,961 --> 00:15:04,011
# Descent.

141
00:15:04,011 --> 00:15:07,799
This movement is the first requisite for vaginal birth.

142
00:15:07,799 --> 00:15:15,936
In nulliparas, engagement may take place before labor onset, and further descent may not follow until second-stage labor.

143
00:15:15,936 --> 00:15:20,074
In multiparas, descent usually begins with engagement.

144
00:15:20,074 --> 00:15:35,599
Descent stems from one or more of three forces: (1) direct myometrial pressure of the fundus upon the breech with contractions, (2) bearing-down efforts of maternal abdominal muscles, and (3) extension and straightening of the fetal body.

145
00:15:35,599 --> 00:15:37,711
# Flexion.

146
00:15:37,711 --> 00:15:45,961
As soon as the descending head meets resistance, whether from the cervix, pelvic walls, or pelvic floor, it normally flexes.

147
00:15:45,961 --> 00:16:00,061
With this movement, the chin draws closer to the fetal thorax, and the appreciably shorter suboccipitobregmatic diameter replaces the longer occipitofrontal diameter (Fig. 29-1, p. 534).

148
00:16:00,061 --> 00:16:06,161
This is an essential requisite for descent because it allows the smallest head diameter to progress.

149
00:16:06,161 --> 00:16:08,786
# Internal Rotation.

150
00:16:08,786 --> 00:16:13,836
This movement turns the occiput gradually away from the transverse axis.

151
00:16:13,836 --> 00:16:18,661
Usually the occiput rotates anteriorly toward the symphysis pubis.

152
00:16:18,661 --> 00:16:24,374
LOT positions transition to left occiput anterior (LOA) positions .

153
00:16:24,374 --> 00:16:29,861
ROT positions rotate to right occiput anterior (ROA) positions.

154
00:16:29,861 --> 00:16:37,874
Less commonly, the head may rotate posteriorly toward the hollow of the sacrum to generate occiput posterior positions.

155
00:16:37,874 --> 00:16:44,286
Internal rotation is essential for completion of labor, except when the fetus is unusually small.

156
00:16:44,286 --> 00:16:46,961
Figure 22-6.

157
00:16:46,961 --> 00:16:52,261
Mechanisms of labor for the left occiput transverse position, lateral view.

158
00:16:52,261 --> 00:16:56,774
A. Engagement with posterior asynclitism at the pelvic brim.

159
00:16:56,774 --> 00:17:01,586
During descent, the sagittal suture is then deflected toward the sacrum.

160
00:17:01,586 --> 00:17:05,286
B.This leads to anterior asynclitism.

161
00:17:05,286 --> 00:17:11,824
This corrects during additional descent C. Internal rotation moves the occiput toward the symphysis.

162
00:17:11,824 --> 00:17:14,486
Farther simultaneous descent.

163
00:17:14,486 --> 00:17:18,049
D. Additional descent with extension of the neck.

164
00:17:18,049 --> 00:17:25,974
Calkins (1939) studied more than 5000 women in labor to ascertain the time of internal rotation.

165
00:17:25,974 --> 00:17:43,149
He concluded that in approximately two thirds, internal rotation is completed by the time the head reaches the pelvic floor; in about another fourth, internal rotation is completed shortly after the head reaches the pelvic floor; and in the remaining 5 percent, rotation does not take place.

166
00:17:43,149 --> 00:17:51,511
When the head fails to turn until reaching the pelvic floor, it typically rotates during the next one or two contractions in multiparas.

167
00:17:51,511 --> 00:17:57,174
In nulliparas, rotation usually occurs during the next three to five contractions.

168
00:17:57,174 --> 00:17:59,374
# Extension.

169
00:17:59,374 --> 00:18:05,674
After internal rotation, the sharply flexed head reaches the vulva and undergoes extension.

170
00:18:05,674 --> 00:18:18,274
If the sharply flexed head, on reaching the pelvic floor, did not extend but was driven farther downward, it would impinge on the posterior portion of the perineum and would eventually be forced through the perineal tissues.

171
00:18:18,274 --> 00:18:23,536
When the head presses on the pelvic floor, however, two forces come into play.

172
00:18:23,536 --> 00:18:33,811
The first force, exerted by the uterus, acts more posteriorly, and the second, supplied by the resistant pelvic floor and the symphysis, acts more anteriorly.

173
00:18:33,811 --> 00:18:39,699
The resultant vector is in the direction of the vulvar opening, thereby causing head extension.

174
00:18:39,699 --> 00:18:48,324
This brings the base of the occiput into direct contact with the inferior margin of the symphysis pubis (see Fig. 22-6).

175
00:18:48,324 --> 00:18:56,336
With progressive distention of the perineum and vaginal opening, an increasingly large portion of the occiput gradually appears.

176
00:18:56,336 --> 00:18:58,449
The head is born as

177
00:18:58,612 --> 00:19:06,549
the occiput, anterior fontanel, brow, nose, mouth, and chin pass successively over the perineal body.

178
00:19:06,499 --> 00:19:12,637
Immediately after its delivery, the head drops so that the chin lies over the maternal anus.

179
00:19:12,637 --> 00:19:15,299
# External Rotation.

180
00:19:15,299 --> 00:19:20,699
The delivered head next undergoes restitution (see Fig. 22-4).

181
00:19:20,699 --> 00:19:28,237
If the occiput was originally directed toward the maternal left, it rotates toward the mother’s left ischial tuberosity.

182
00:19:28,237 --> 00:19:33,412
If it was originally directed toward the right, the occiput rotates to the right.

183
00:19:33,412 --> 00:19:37,649
With restitution, the head reaches a transverse position.

184
00:19:37,649 --> 00:19:46,937
The fetal body aligns its bisacromial diameter, which is the distance across the shoulders, with the anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic outlet.

185
00:19:46,937 --> 00:19:52,587
Thus, one shoulder is anterior behind the symphysis and the other is posterior.

186
00:19:52,587 --> 00:19:54,812
# Expulsion.

187
00:19:54,812 --> 00:20:05,524
Almost immediately after external rotation, the anterior shoulder appears under the symphysis pubis, and the perineum soon becomes distended by the posterior shoulder.

188
00:20:05,524 --> 00:20:10,187
After delivery of the shoulders, the rest of the body quickly passes.

189
00:20:10,187 --> 00:20:20,062
If the anterior shoulder tightly wedges behind the symphysis, shoulder dystocia is diagnosed and is described in Chapter 27 (p. 501).

190
00:20:20,062 --> 00:20:23,324
# Occiput Posterior Position.

191
00:20:23,324 --> 00:20:31,037
In approximately 20 percent of labors, the fetus enters the pelvis in an occiput posterior (OP) position .

192
00:20:31,037 --> 00:20:38,612
It appears likely from radiographic evidence that posterior positions are more often associated with a narrow forepelvis .

193
00:20:38,612 --> 00:20:51,999
Effective contractions, adequate head flexion, and average fetal size together permit most posteriorly positioned occiputs to rotate anteriorly toward the symphysis promptly as soon as they reach the pelvic floor.

194
00:20:51,999 --> 00:21:01,512
In perhaps 5 to 10 percent of cases, however, rotation may be incomplete or may not take place at all, especially if the fetus is large .

195
00:21:01,512 --> 00:21:13,699
Poor contractions, faulty head flexion, or epidural analgesia, which diminishes maternal muscular pushing and relaxes pelvic floor muscles, may predispose to incomplete rotation.

196
00:21:13,699 --> 00:21:18,137
If rotation is incomplete, transverse arrest may result.

197
00:21:18,137 --> 00:21:27,762
If no rotation toward the symphysis proceeds, the occiput may remain in the direct OP position, a condition known as persistent occiput posterior.

198
00:21:27,762 --> 00:21:31,462
Both can lead to dystocia and cesarean delivery.

199
00:21:31,462 --> 00:21:38,949
Risk factors and labor management of a persistent OP position are found in Chapter 27 (p. 500).

200
00:21:38,949 --> 00:21:46,637
Techniques to manually rotate from OP to OA positions are illustrated in Chapter 29 (p. 540).

201
00:21:46,637 --> 00:21:49,699
# Fetal Head Shape Changes.

202
00:21:49,699 --> 00:21:54,649
In occiput presentations, labor forces alter fetal head shape.

203
00:21:54,649 --> 00:22:03,487
In prolonged labors before complete cervical dilation, the portion of the fetal scalp immediately over the cervical os becomes edematous.

204
00:22:03,487 --> 00:22:06,787
This swelling is called caput succedaneum.

205
00:22:06,787 --> 00:22:17,749
It usually attains a thickness of only a few millimeters, but in prolonged labors it may be sufficiently extensive to prevent differentiation of the various sutures and fontanels.

206
00:22:17,749 --> 00:22:27,862
More commonly, the caput is formed when the head is in the lower portion of the birth canal and frequently only after the resistance of a rigid vaginal outlet is encountered.

207
00:22:27,862 --> 00:22:34,587
Because it develops over the most dependent area of the head, one may deduce the original fetal head position.

208
00:22:34,587 --> 00:22:42,012
In cases of marked asynclitism and dystocia, the caput succedaneum may form far from the sagittal midline .

209
00:22:42,012 --> 00:22:44,637
Figure 22-7.

210
00:22:44,637 --> 00:22:48,549
Fetal head molding and caput succedaneum formation.

211
00:22:48,549 --> 00:22:54,849
A. This newborn was delivered by cesarean for failure to progress and active labor arrest.

212
00:22:54,849 --> 00:23:04,424
The obvious caput succedaneum, which developed far from and to the left of the sagittal midline (arrow), reflects marked asynclitism during labor.

213
00:23:04,424 --> 00:23:10,924
B. This neonate after vaginal birth shows significant caput succedaneum and elongated molding.

214
00:23:10,924 --> 00:23:19,662
Molding refers to changes in the bony fetal head shape as a result of external compressive forces (see Fig. 22-7B).

215
00:23:19,662 --> 00:23:25,449
Possibly related to Braxton Hicks contractions, some molding develops before labor.

216
00:23:25,449 --> 00:23:31,699
Despite these shape changes, most studies indicate that the parietal bones seldom overlap.

217
00:23:31,699 --> 00:23:37,687
A “locking” mechanism at the coronal and lambdoidal sutures actually prevents such overlapping .

218
00:23:37,687 --> 00:23:41,887
Molding yields a shortened suboccipitobregmatic diameter.

219
00:23:41,887 --> 00:23:43,599
Of greatest

220
00:23:43,756 --> 00:23:53,431
importance in women with contracted pelves or asynclitism, the degree to which the head can mold may determine the difference between vaginal and cesarean birth.

221
00:23:53,381 --> 00:23:59,543
Some older literature implicated severe head molding as a cause for possible cerebral trauma.

222
00:23:59,543 --> 00:24:12,043
Because of the many associated factors, for example, prolonged labor with fetal sepsis and acidosis, it is impossible to link molding to any alleged fetal or neonatal neurological sequelae.

223
00:24:12,043 --> 00:24:18,406
Most molding resolves within the week following delivery, although persistent cases have been described .

224
00:24:18,406 --> 00:24:26,981
Differentiation of molding, caput succedaneum, and cephalohematoma is discussed in Chapter 33 (p. 608).

225
00:24:26,981 --> 00:24:29,643
Normal Labor Characteristics.

226
00:24:29,643 --> 00:24:34,831
The greatest impediment to understanding normal labor is recognizing its start.

227
00:24:34,831 --> 00:24:41,981
The strict definition describes uterine contractions that bring about demonstrable cervical effacement and dilation.

228
00:24:41,981 --> 00:24:48,943
Pinpointing when labor actually begins, however, is challenging because determination is retrospective.

229
00:24:48,943 --> 00:24:52,106
Several methods are used to mark its start.

230
00:24:52,106 --> 00:25:00,856
Despite these, a recent systematic review of labor definitions emphasizes the lack of consensus regarding definitions of labor onset .

231
00:25:00,856 --> 00:25:06,518
One method defines onset as the clock time when painful contractions become regular.

232
00:25:06,518 --> 00:25:14,731
However, uterine activity that causes discomfort but that does not represent true labor may develop at any time during pregnancy.

233
00:25:14,731 --> 00:25:23,356
False labor eventually wanes spontaneously, whereas the contractions of true labor typically progress to an effective, regular pattern.

234
00:25:23,356 --> 00:25:29,531
A second method defines the onset of labor as beginning at the time of admission to a labor unit.

235
00:25:29,531 --> 00:25:37,806
In the United States, admission for labor is frequently based on the extent of cervical dilation accompanied by painful contractions.

236
00:25:37,806 --> 00:25:48,731
If a woman has intact membranes, a cervical dilation of 3 to 4 cm or greater and frequent painful contractions are reasonably reliable diagnostic criteria.

237
00:25:48,731 --> 00:25:51,518
# First Stage of Labor.

238
00:25:51,518 --> 00:26:00,631
For labor progression, Friedman (1954) described a characteristic sigmoid pattern by graphing cervical dilation against time.

239
00:26:00,631 --> 00:26:09,443
With Friedman’s concept, the first stage of labor contains three functional labor divisions, each with its own physiological characteristics .

240
00:26:09,443 --> 00:26:17,506
First, during the preparatory division, although the cervix dilates little, its connective tissue components change considerably .

241
00:26:17,506 --> 00:26:22,981
Sedation and conduction analgesia are capable of arresting this labor division.

242
00:26:22,981 --> 00:26:30,343
The dilatational division, during which dilation proceeds at its most rapid rate, is unaffected by sedation.

243
00:26:30,343 --> 00:26:36,131
Last, the pelvic division commences with the deceleration phase of cervical dilation.

244
00:26:36,131 --> 00:26:41,193
The cardinal movements of labor take place principally during this pelvic division.

245
00:26:41,193 --> 00:26:47,756
In actual practice, however, the onset of the pelvic division is seldom clearly identifiable.

246
00:26:47,756 --> 00:26:50,193
Figure 22-8.

247
00:26:50,193 --> 00:27:13,606
Labor progress is described by dilatation and descent curves and is partitioned into: (1) a preparatory division, including latent and acceleration phases; (2) a dilatational division, which contains the phase of maximum slope; and (3) a pelvic division, which encompasses both the deceleration phase and second-stage labor as well as the phase of maximum slope of descent.

248
00:27:13,606 --> 00:27:17,068
Two phases of cervical dilation are defined.

249
00:27:17,068 --> 00:27:23,643
The latent phase corresponds to the preparatory division, and the active phase to the dilational division.

250
00:27:23,643 --> 00:27:32,243
Friedman further subdivided the active phase into the acceleration phase, the phase of maximum slope, and the deceleration phase .

251
00:27:32,243 --> 00:27:34,843
Figure 22-9.

252
00:27:34,843 --> 00:27:39,243
Composite of the average dilatation curve for nulliparous labor.

253
00:27:39,243 --> 00:27:46,006
The first stage is divided into a relatively flat latent phase and a rapidly progressive active phase.

254
00:27:46,006 --> 00:27:52,981
The active phase contains an acceleration phase, a phase of maximum slope, and a deceleration phase.

255
00:27:52,981 --> 00:28:02,618
Latent Phase The onset of latent labor, as defined by Friedman (1972), is the point at which the mother perceives regular contractions.

256
00:28:02,618 --> 00:28:08,543
For most women, this phase ends once cervical dilation of 4 cm is achieved.

257
00:28:08,543 --> 00:28:15,943
This threshold may be clinically useful, for it defines dilation limits beyond which active labor can be expected.

258
00:28:15,943 --> 00:28:18,656
More recently, the American

259
00:28:18,820 --> 00:28:29,595
College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2019c) has redefined active labor to begin at 6 cm.

260
00:28:29,545 --> 00:28:35,907
A fuller discussion of these labor changes is found in Chapter 23 (p. 436).

261
00:28:35,907 --> 00:28:46,070
A prolonged latent phase was defined as one exceeding 20 hours in the nullipara and 14 hours in the multipara by Friedman and Sachtleben (1963).

262
00:28:46,070 --> 00:28:50,245
These times corresponded to the 95th percentiles.

263
00:28:50,245 --> 00:29:02,795
Factors that affected latent phase duration include excessive sedation or epidural analgesia; unfavorable cervical condition, that is, thick, uneffaced, or undilated; and false labor.

264
00:29:02,795 --> 00:29:10,082
Of women in latent labor who had been administered heavy sedation, 85 percent eventually entered active labor.

265
00:29:10,082 --> 00:29:15,670
In another 10 percent, uterine contractions ceased, which suggests false labor.

266
00:29:15,670 --> 00:29:22,707
In the remaining 5 percent, an abnormal latent phase persisted and required oxytocin stimulation.

267
00:29:22,707 --> 00:29:27,745
Amniotomy was discouraged because of the 10-percent incidence of false labor.

268
00:29:27,745 --> 00:29:37,857
Friedman (1972) later reported that latent phase prolongation did not adversely influence fetal or maternal morbidity or mortality rates.

269
00:29:37,857 --> 00:29:52,832
However, prolongation of the latent phase appears associated with longer active labor duration and greater likelihood of obstetrical interventions (Ängeby, 2018; Chelmow, 1993; Tilden, 2020).

270
00:29:52,832 --> 00:30:06,532
Active Phase The progress of labor in nulliparas has particular significance because these curves all reveal a rapid change in the slope of cervical dilation rates between 3 and 5 cm (see Fig. 22-9).

271
00:30:06,532 --> 00:30:17,282
Thus, cervical dilation of 3 to 6 cm or more, in the presence of uterine contractions, can be taken to reliably represent the threshold for active labor.

272
00:30:17,282 --> 00:30:22,470
Similarly, these curves provide useful guideposts for labor management.

273
00:30:22,470 --> 00:30:30,732
Turning again to Friedman (1955), the mean duration of active-phase labor in nulliparas was 4.9 hours.

274
00:30:30,732 --> 00:30:40,970
But, the standard deviation of 3.4 hours is large, hence, the active phase was reported to have a statistical maximum of 11.7 hours.

275
00:30:40,970 --> 00:30:58,582
Indeed, rates of cervical dilation ranged from a minimum of 1.2 up to 6.8 cm/hr. Friedman (1972) also found that multiparas progress somewhat faster in active-phase labor, with a minimum normal rate of 1.5 cm/hr.

276
00:30:58,582 --> 00:31:07,257
His analysis of active-phase labor concomitantly describes rates of fetal descent and cervical dilation (see Fig. 22-8).

277
00:31:07,257 --> 00:31:16,832
Descent begins in the later stage of active dilation, commencing at 7 to 8 cm in nulliparas and becoming most rapid after 8 cm.

278
00:31:16,832 --> 00:31:20,257
Others have reassessed the Friedman labor curves.

279
00:31:20,257 --> 00:31:31,432
Zhang and associates (2010) studied electronic labor records from 62,415 parturients with spontaneous labor at term and vaginal birth.

280
00:31:31,432 --> 00:31:46,770
For primiparas, the median time to progress from 4 to 5 cm was 1.3 hours, from 5 to 6 cm 0.8 hours, and thereafter, additional centimeters were gained approximately each 0.5 hours.

281
00:31:46,770 --> 00:31:57,320
They found that normal labor may take >6 hours to progress from 4 to 5 cm and >3 hours to progress from 5 to 6 cm dilation.

282
00:31:57,320 --> 00:32:06,145
Rates for multiparas were similar to nulliparas from 4 to 6 cm, but then, labor accelerated much faster in multiparas.

283
00:32:06,145 --> 00:32:20,520
Data from this study form the foundation for new guidelines regarding cesarean delivery indications for labor arrest put forth in the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists’ Obstetric Care Consensus (2019c).

284
00:32:20,520 --> 00:32:25,107
These are described in Chapter 23 (p. 436).

285
00:32:25,107 --> 00:32:27,932
# Second Stage of Labor.

286
00:32:27,932 --> 00:32:33,257
This stage begins with complete cervical dilation and ends with fetal delivery.

287
00:32:33,257 --> 00:32:41,532
The median duration is approximately 50 minutes for nulliparas and about 20 minutes for multiparas, but it is highly variable .

288
00:32:41,532 --> 00:32:52,082
In a woman of higher parity with a previously dilated vagina and perineum, two or three expulsive efforts after full cervical dilation may suffice to complete delivery.

289
00:32:52,082 --> 00:33:04,245
Conversely, in a woman with a contracted pelvis, with a large fetus, or with impaired expulsive efforts from conduction analgesia or sedation, the second stage may become abnormally long.

290
00:33:04,245 --> 00:33:09,695
Nulliparas with high starting fetal station may experience a longer second stage .

291
00:33:09,695 --> 00:33:19,120
Greater maternal body mass index does not interfere with secondstage labor (Carlhäll, 2013; Robinson, 2011).

292
00:33:19,120 --> 00:33:21,107
# Labor

293
00:33:21,268 --> 00:33:22,905
Duration.

294
00:33:22,855 --> 00:33:30,943
The normal duration of labor may be clouded by the many clinical variables that affect the conduct of labor in modern obstetrical units.

295
00:33:30,943 --> 00:33:45,630
Kilpatrick and Laros (1989) reported that the mean length of first- and second-stage labors approximates 9 hours in nulliparas without regional analgesia and that the 95th percentile upper limit was 18.5 hours.

296
00:33:45,630 --> 00:33:53,955
Corresponding times for multiparas were a mean of 6 hours and a 95th percentile maximum of 13.5 hours.

297
00:33:53,955 --> 00:34:03,555
These authors defined labor onset as the time when a woman recalled regular, painful contractions every 3 to 5 minutes that led to cervical change.

298
00:34:03,555 --> 00:34:12,043
Spontaneous labor was analyzed in nearly 25,000 women delivered at term at Parkland Hospital in the early 1990s.

299
00:34:12,043 --> 00:34:26,818
Almost 80 percent of women were admitted with a cervical dilation of ≤5 cm. Parity—nulliparous versus multiparous—and cervical dilation at admission were significant determinants of spontaneous labor length.

300
00:34:26,818 --> 00:34:37,555
The median time from admission to spontaneous delivery for all parturients was 3.5 hours, and 95 percent of all women delivered within 10.1 hours.

301
00:34:37,555 --> 00:34:42,668
These results suggest that normal human active labor is relatively short.

302
00:34:42,668 --> 00:34:45,205
Management Of Normal Labor.

303
00:34:45,205 --> 00:34:51,330
Birthing is a normal physiological process that most women experience without complications.

304
00:34:51,330 --> 00:34:56,330
However, unexpected intrapartum complications can arise quickly.

305
00:34:56,330 --> 00:35:06,355
Thus, every woman and her supporters should feel welcomed and comfortable, yet safety measures must be in place for the mother and her newborn if complications suddenly develop.

306
00:35:06,355 --> 00:35:18,155
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) have collaborated in the development of Guidelines for Perinatal Care.

307
00:35:18,155 --> 00:35:26,993
These provide detailed information on the appropriate content of intrapartum care, including both personnel and facility requirements.

308
00:35:26,993 --> 00:35:32,043
Labor and delivery outside the hospital is elected by some parturients.

309
00:35:32,043 --> 00:35:37,018
This option is discussed in Chapter 27 (p. 504).

310
00:35:37,018 --> 00:35:41,468
# Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act—EMTALA.

311
00:35:41,468 --> 00:35:55,043
By law, all Medicare-participating hospitals with emergency services must provide an appropriate screening examination for any pregnant woman experiencing contractions and presenting to the emergency department for evaluation.

312
00:35:55,043 --> 00:36:01,993
The definition of an emergency condition makes specific reference to a pregnant woman who is having contractions.

313
00:36:01,993 --> 00:36:09,393
Labor is defined as “the process of childbirth beginning with the latent phase of labor continuing through delivery of the placenta.

314
00:36:09,393 --> 00:36:18,705
A woman experiencing contractions is in true labor unless a physician certifies that after a reasonable time of observation the woman is in false labor.

315
00:36:18,705 --> 00:36:26,693
A woman in true labor is considered “unstable” for interhospital transfer purposes until the newborn and placenta are delivered.

316
00:36:26,693 --> 00:36:37,980
A stable woman may, however, be transferred at the direction of the patient or by a physician who certifies that the benefits of treatment at another facility outweigh the transfer risks.

317
00:36:37,980 --> 00:36:40,893
# Identification of Labor.

318
00:36:40,893 --> 00:36:47,505
Pregnant women are urged to report early in labor rather than to procrastinate until delivery is imminent.

319
00:36:47,505 --> 00:36:56,755
Early admittance is important if during antepartum care, the woman, her fetus, or both are found to carry risks for intrapartum complications.

320
00:36:56,755 --> 00:37:08,318
Although the differentiation between false and true labor is difficult at times, the diagnosis usually can be clarified by cervical dilation and by contraction frequency and intensity.

321
00:37:08,318 --> 00:37:15,418
Pates and associates (2007) studied one commonly used recommendation given to pregnant women.

322
00:37:15,418 --> 00:37:28,618
Namely, in the absence of ruptured membranes or bleeding, uterine contractions 5 minutes apart for 1 hour—that is, ≥12 contractions in 1 hour—may signify labor onset.

323
00:37:28,618 --> 00:37:44,718
Among 768 women in this study at Parkland Hospital, active labor defined as cervical dilation ≥4 cm was diagnosed within 24 hours in three fourths of women with ≥12 contractions per hour.

324
00:37:44,718 --> 00:37:52,955
In those instances when a diagnosis of labor cannot be established with certainty, observation for a longer period is reasonable.

325
00:37:52,955 --> 00:38:00,568
Women who present to Parkland Hospital for labor symptoms at ≥240/7 weeks gestation

326
00:38:00,724 --> 00:38:07,474
are routinely evaluated in a labor triage unit, which is contiguous to our labor and delivery unit.

327
00:38:07,424 --> 00:38:15,299
All women in the triage unit are evaluated by nurse practitioners and certified nurse midwives using written protocols.

328
00:38:15,299 --> 00:38:27,011
For those with uncomplicated pregnancies, women with intact membranes and cervical dilation <4 cm receive continuous external fetal monitoring for up to 2 hours.

329
00:38:27,011 --> 00:38:33,574
Women diagnosed with labor by either cervical change or persistent uterine contractions are admitted.

330
00:38:33,574 --> 00:38:42,199
After review by a physician, women without cervical change or with abatement of contractions return home with a diagnosis of false labor.

331
00:38:42,199 --> 00:38:54,661
In a recent study, a total of 3949 women with uncomplicated pregnancies between 370/7 and 416/7 weeks’ gestation were diagnosed with false labor.

332
00:38:54,661 --> 00:39:00,561
The mean interval from hospital discharge to when they again presented was 4.9 days .

333
00:39:00,561 --> 00:39:10,274
Within this protocol, hospital discharge with false labor at term was not associated with higher rates of adverse neonatal outcomes or cesarean delivery.

334
00:39:10,274 --> 00:39:19,074
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) has endorsed hospital-based obstetrical triage units.

335
00:39:19,074 --> 00:39:23,836
Instead of false labor, labor in the latent phase may be diagnosed.

336
00:39:23,836 --> 00:39:28,636
In this phase, some women may require admission for pain management.

337
00:39:28,636 --> 00:39:37,836
However, for women without antepartum risks and with reassuring maternofetal status, expectant care during latent labor is reasonable .

338
00:39:37,836 --> 00:39:52,274
Studies support the value of this approach to potentially lower rates of interventions that include oxytocin, antibiotics for intrapartum fever, and cesarean delivery (Bailiet, 2005; Wood, 2016).

339
00:39:52,274 --> 00:39:56,761
Instructions on coping and self-care during this time have value .

340
00:39:56,761 --> 00:40:01,699
Moreover, a mutually agreed upon time is set for reevaluation.

341
00:40:01,699 --> 00:40:04,486
# Initial Evaluation.

342
00:40:04,486 --> 00:40:12,211
For the gravida presenting with symptoms of labor, blood pressure, temperature, pulse, and respiratory rate are recorded.

343
00:40:12,211 --> 00:40:18,636
Fetal heart rate is evaluated using a portable Doppler device, sonography, or fetoscope.

344
00:40:18,636 --> 00:40:24,686
Our practice uses extended electronic fetal monitoring to assess initial fetal status.

345
00:40:24,686 --> 00:40:29,436
The pregnancy record is promptly reviewed to identify complications.

346
00:40:29,436 --> 00:40:35,536
A vaginal examination is performed unless there is a known placenta previa or vasa previa.

347
00:40:35,536 --> 00:40:41,549
The gloved index and second fingers are introduced into the vagina while avoiding the anal region.

348
00:40:41,549 --> 00:40:50,086
Ruptured Membranes During prenatal care, the woman is instructed to be aware of fluid leakage from the vagina and to report this event promptly.

349
00:40:50,086 --> 00:40:54,111
Rupture of the membranes is significant for three reasons.

350
00:40:54,111 --> 00:41:01,161
First, if the presenting part is not fixed in the pelvis, the umbilical cord can prolapse and be compressed.

351
00:41:01,161 --> 00:41:06,549
Second, labor is likely to begin soon if the pregnancy is at or near term.

352
00:41:06,549 --> 00:41:15,486
Third, if delivery is delayed after membrane rupture, intrauterine and neonatal infection is more likely as the time interval lengthens .

353
00:41:15,486 --> 00:41:26,024
During sterile speculum examination, ruptured membranes are diagnosed if amnionic fluid pools in the posterior fornix or clear fluid flows from the cervical canal.

354
00:41:26,024 --> 00:41:32,586
If the diagnosis remains uncertain, one method involves pH determination of vaginal fluid.

355
00:41:32,586 --> 00:41:43,249
The pH of vaginal secretions normally ranges from 4.5 to 5.5, whereas the pH of amnionic fluid is usually >7.0.

356
00:41:43,249 --> 00:41:50,061
The use of the indicator nitrazine to identify ruptured membranes is a simple and fairly reliable method.

357
00:41:50,061 --> 00:42:01,836
Test papers are impregnated with the dye, and the color of the reaction between these paper strips and vaginal fluids is interpreted by comparison with a standard color chart posted on the strip’s container.

358
00:42:01,836 --> 00:42:06,649
A pH above 6.5 is consistent with ruptured membranes.

359
00:42:06,649 --> 00:42:13,924
False-positive test results may stem from blood, semen, or bacterial vaginosis, which raise pH.

360
00:42:13,924 --> 00:42:17,799
Scant fluid can yield a false-negative test result.

361
00:42:17,799 --> 00:42:25,174
Another test to identify amnionic fluid microscopically evaluates vaginal fluid that is dried on a slide.

362
00:42:25,174 --> 00:42:30,624
Arborization or a fern pattern suggests amnionic rather than cervical fluid.

363
00:42:30,624 --> 00:42:38,736
Amnionic fluid crystallizes and ferns due to its concentrations of sodium chloride, proteins, and carbohydrates.

364
00:42:38,736 --> 00:42:43,849
Of other methods, detection of alpha-fetoprotein in the vaginal vault has

365
00:42:44,020 --> 00:42:47,307
been used to identify amnionic fluid .

366
00:42:47,257 --> 00:42:59,332
Rarely required, identification may also follow injection of 1 to 4 mL of a 5-percent sodium fluorescein solution into the amnionic sac via abdominal amniocentesis .

367
00:42:59,332 --> 00:43:05,270
Last, specific amnionic fluid proteins can be sought using point-of-care assays.

368
00:43:05,270 --> 00:43:25,707
These include AmniSure, which binds placental alpha microglobulin1; Actim PROM, with binds insulin growth factor binding protein-1 (IGFBP1); and ROM Plus, which detects IGFBP-1 plus alpha-fetoprotein (Eleje, 2017; Palacio, 2014).

369
00:43:25,707 --> 00:43:29,920
False-positive and false-negative results are not uncommon.

370
00:43:29,920 --> 00:43:38,282
The Food and Drug Administration (2018) emphasizes the need to incorporate clinical findings if these methods are used.

371
00:43:38,282 --> 00:43:43,632
Cervical Assessment Progress during labor produces specific cervical changes.

372
00:43:43,632 --> 00:43:52,207
Cervical dilation is assessed by sweeping the examining finger from the inner cervical rim on one side of the opening to the opposite inner rim.

373
00:43:52,207 --> 00:43:56,107
The diameter traversed is estimated in centimeters.

374
00:43:56,107 --> 00:44:03,545
Effective labor will dilate the cervix, which is considered fully dilated when the diameter measures 10 cm.

375
00:44:03,545 --> 00:44:09,645
The presenting part of a term-size newborn usually can pass through a cervix this widely dilated.

376
00:44:09,645 --> 00:44:15,707
Cervical effacement is the length of the cervical canal compared with that of an unlabored cervix.

377
00:44:15,707 --> 00:44:20,970
When the length of the cervix is reduced by one half, it is 50-percent effaced.

378
00:44:20,970 --> 00:44:28,807
When the cervix becomes as thin as the adjacent lower uterine segment, it is completely, or 100-percent, effaced.

379
00:44:28,807 --> 00:44:35,245
Cervical position reflects the relationship of the cervical os to the center point of the presenting part.

380
00:44:35,245 --> 00:44:40,057
It is categorized as posterior, midposition, or anterior.

381
00:44:40,057 --> 00:44:44,170
Fetal descent will gradually move the cervix anteriorly.

382
00:44:44,170 --> 00:44:51,820
Along with position, the consistency of the cervix is determined to be soft, firm, or intermediate between these two.

383
00:44:51,820 --> 00:44:55,520
Cervical softening aids dilation and effacement.

384
00:44:55,520 --> 00:45:03,007
Fetal station describes the presenting fetal part’s leading edge in the birth canal in relationship to the ischial spines.

385
00:45:03,007 --> 00:45:09,795
These spines lie halfway between the pelvic inlet and the pelvic outlet and lie at the level of the midpelvis.

386
00:45:09,795 --> 00:45:18,057
When the lowermost portion of the presenting fetal part reaches the spines, it is designated as being at zero (0) station.

387
00:45:18,057 --> 00:45:30,220
In the past, the long axis of the birth canal above and below the ischial spines was arbitrarily divided into thirds by some and into fifths (approximately 1 cm) by other groups.

388
00:45:30,220 --> 00:45:41,182
In 1989, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists adopted the classification of station that divides the pelvis above and below the spines into fifths.

389
00:45:41,182 --> 00:45:45,870
Each fifth represents 1 cm above or below the spines.

390
00:45:45,870 --> 00:45:58,420
Thus, as the presenting fetal part descends from the inlet toward the ischial spines, the designation is –5, –4, – 3, –2, –1, and then 0 station.

391
00:45:58,420 --> 00:46:08,970
Below the spines, as the presenting fetal part descends, it passes +1, +2, +3, +4, and +5 stations to delivery.

392
00:46:08,970 --> 00:46:14,945
Station +5 cm corresponds to the fetal head being visible at the introitus.

393
00:46:14,945 --> 00:46:25,557
If the leading part of the fetal head is at 0 station or below, most often the fetal head has engaged—thus, the biparietal plane has passed through the pelvic inlet.

394
00:46:25,557 --> 00:46:36,157
If the head is unusually molded or if caput succedaneum formation is extensive, or both, engagement might not have taken place although the scalp is at 0 station.

395
00:46:36,157 --> 00:46:48,470
In addition to charting the progress of labor, these five characteristics— cervical dilation, effacement, consistency, position, and fetal station—are assessed when tabulating the Bishop score.

396
00:46:48,470 --> 00:46:56,270
This score is commonly used to predict labor induction outcome and is discussed in Chapter 26 (p. 488).

397
00:46:56,270 --> 00:47:03,182
Taken together, these factors suggest the subjective “favorability” of the cervix for induction success.

398
00:47:03,182 --> 00:47:11,057
Laboratory Studies When a woman is admitted in labor, most often the hematocrit or hemoglobin concentration is rechecked.

399
00:47:11,057 --> 00:47:14,745
The hematocrit can be measured easily and quickly.

400
00:47:14,745 --> 00:47:20,732
At Parkland Hospital, blood is collected in a standard collection tube with anticoagulant.

401
00:47:20,732 --> 00:47:28,457
From this, a heparinized capillary tube is filled to spin in a microhematocrit centrifuge in the labor and delivery unit.

402
00:47:28,457 --> 00:47:29,920
This

403
00:47:30,076 --> 00:47:33,713
provides a hematocrit value within 3 minutes.

404
00:47:33,663 --> 00:47:42,863
The initial collection tube is also sent to the hematology laboratory for evaluation if the point-of-care hematocrit is <30 volume percent.

405
00:47:42,863 --> 00:47:49,363
Another labeled tube of blood is allowed to clot and sent to the blood bank for blood type and antibody screen.

406
00:47:49,363 --> 00:48:02,251
Some states, for example, Texas, require routine testing for syphilis, hepatitis B, and HIV in all women admitted to labor and delivery units, even if these were done during prenatal care.

407
00:48:02,251 --> 00:48:09,101
In some labor units, a clean-catch voided specimen is examined in all women for protein and glucose.

408
00:48:09,101 --> 00:48:16,113
At Parkland Hospital, we obtain a urine specimen for protein determination in hypertensive women only .

409
00:48:16,113 --> 00:48:19,426
# Management of First-stage Labor.

410
00:48:19,426 --> 00:48:25,338
As soon as possible after admittance, the remainder of a general examination is completed.

411
00:48:25,338 --> 00:48:33,713
Whether a pregnancy is normal can best be determined when all examinations, including record and laboratory review, are completed.

412
00:48:33,713 --> 00:48:40,101
A rational plan for monitoring labor can then be established based on the needs of the fetus and the mother.

413
00:48:40,101 --> 00:48:49,076
Because labor lengths vary markedly among individuals, precise statements to the patient regarding anticipated labor duration are unwise.

414
00:48:49,076 --> 00:48:54,363
Intrapartum Fetal Monitoring This is discussed in detail in Chapter 24.

415
00:48:54,363 --> 00:49:15,501
Briefly, the American Academy of Pediatrics and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend that during first-stage labor, in the absence of any abnormalities, the fetal heart rate should be checked immediately after a contraction at least every 30 minutes and then every 15 minutes during second-stage labor.

416
00:49:15,501 --> 00:49:26,276
If continuous electronic monitoring is used, the tracing is evaluated at least every 30 minutes during the first stage and at least every 15 minutes during the second stage.

417
00:49:26,276 --> 00:49:36,901
For women with pregnancies at risk, fetal heart auscultation is performed at least every 15 minutes during first-stage labor and every 5 minutes during the second stage.

418
00:49:36,901 --> 00:49:47,588
Continuous electronic monitoring may be used with evaluation of the tracing every 15 minutes during the first stage of labor, and every 5 minutes during the second stage.

419
00:49:47,588 --> 00:49:54,301
Maternal Monitoring Temperature, pulse, and blood pressure are evaluated at least every 4 hours.

420
00:49:54,301 --> 00:50:01,926
If membranes have been ruptured for many hours or if there is a borderline temperature elevation, the temperature is checked hourly.

421
00:50:01,926 --> 00:50:10,813
Although uterine contractions are usually assessed with electronic monitoring, they can be quantitatively and qualitatively evaluated manually.

422
00:50:10,813 --> 00:50:17,201
With the palm of the hand resting lightly on the uterus, the time of contraction onset is determined.

423
00:50:17,201 --> 00:50:22,076
Its intensity is gauged from the degree of muscle tone the uterus achieves.

424
00:50:22,076 --> 00:50:29,588
At the acme of effective contractions, the finger or thumb cannot readily indent the uterus during a “firm” contraction.

425
00:50:29,588 --> 00:50:33,563
The time at which the contraction disappears is noted next.

426
00:50:33,563 --> 00:50:40,163
This sequence is repeated to evaluate the frequency, duration, and intensity of contractions.

427
00:50:40,163 --> 00:50:49,788
During the first stage of labor, the need for subsequent vaginal examinations to monitor cervical change and presenting part position will vary considerably.

428
00:50:49,788 --> 00:51:00,326
When the membranes rupture, an examination to exclude cord prolapse is performed expeditiously if the fetal head was not definitely engaged at the previous examination.

429
00:51:00,326 --> 00:51:08,413
The fetal heart rate is also checked immediately and during the next uterine contraction to help detect occult umbilical cord compression.

430
00:51:08,413 --> 00:51:19,213
At Parkland Hospital, periodic pelvic examinations are typically performed at 2- to 3-hour intervals for gravidas in active labor to evaluate labor progress.

431
00:51:19,213 --> 00:51:29,901
Evidence implicating the number of vaginal examinations in infection-related morbidity is conflicting (Cahill, 2012; Soper, 1989).

432
00:51:29,901 --> 00:51:38,588
Maternal Position In bed, the laboring woman may assume the position she finds most comfortable, and often this will be lateral recumbency.

433
00:51:38,588 --> 00:51:45,876
Lying supine is typically avoided to avert aortocaval compression and its potential to lower uterine perfusion .

434
00:51:45,876 --> 00:51:51,201
However, the normal laboring woman need not be confined to bed early in labor.

435
00:51:51,201 --> 00:51:55,051
A comfortable chair may be beneficial psychologically

436
00:51:55,204 --> 00:51:57,816
and perhaps physiologically.

437
00:51:57,766 --> 00:52:00,316
Others encourage ambulation.

438
00:52:00,316 --> 00:52:15,954
Proponents of walking report that it shortens labor, lowers rates of oxytocin augmentation, diminishes the need for analgesia, and decreases the frequency of operative vaginal delivery (Flynn, 1978; Read, 1981).

439
00:52:15,954 --> 00:52:30,704
In their Cochrane review, Lawrence and associates (2013) reported that labor in ambulant or upright positions shortened first-stage labor by approximately 1 hour and lowered cesarean delivery and epidural analgesia rates.

440
00:52:30,704 --> 00:52:35,566
However, they cautioned interpretation given variable study quality.

441
00:52:35,566 --> 00:52:46,591
Lupe and Gross (1986) concluded, however, that no conclusive evidence supports assertions that upright maternal posture or ambulation improves labor.

442
00:52:46,591 --> 00:52:51,179
They reported that women preferred to lie on their side or sit in bed.

443
00:52:51,179 --> 00:52:56,341
Few chose to walk, fewer to squat, and none wanted the knee-chest position.

444
00:52:56,341 --> 00:53:00,704
Parturients tended to assume fetal positions in later labor.

445
00:53:00,704 --> 00:53:10,104
Most women enthusiastic about ambulation returned to bed when active labor began (Carlson, 1986; Williams, 1980).

446
00:53:10,104 --> 00:53:18,191
Bloom and colleagues (1998) conducted a randomized trial to study the effects of walking during first-stage labor.

447
00:53:18,191 --> 00:53:28,716
In 1067 women with uncomplicated term pregnancies delivered at Parkland Hospital, these investigators reported that ambulation did not affect labor duration.

448
00:53:28,716 --> 00:53:34,266
Ambulation did not reduce the need for analgesia nor was it harmful to the newborn.

449
00:53:34,266 --> 00:53:43,366
Because of these observations, we give women without complications the option to select either recumbency or supervised ambulation during labor.

450
00:53:43,366 --> 00:53:49,616
Pain Management In general, the needs and desires for pain relief are directed by the parturient.

451
00:53:49,616 --> 00:53:57,779
Relaxation techniques may have a role for pain relief and patient satisfaction, although evidence-based data are not robust .

452
00:53:57,779 --> 00:54:02,104
Discussed in the last section, ambulation is another option.

453
00:54:02,104 --> 00:54:09,104
Some women choose to spend part of first-stage labor in a large water tub, if available, for pain relief.

454
00:54:09,104 --> 00:54:19,866
With this practice, one Cochrane review found lower rates of regional analgesia use and no greater adverse neonatal or maternal effects compared with traditional labor .

455
00:54:19,866 --> 00:54:35,504
Pharmacologic options include epidural analgesia; intermittent intravenous (IV) or intramuscular (IM) opioids or meperidine (Table 25-2, p. 469), and less often, nitrous oxide.

456
00:54:35,504 --> 00:54:39,304
These are discussed in detail in Chapter 25.

457
00:54:39,304 --> 00:54:45,791
Oral Intake Food and liquids with particulate matter should be withheld during active labor and delivery.

458
00:54:45,791 --> 00:54:52,604
Gastric emptying time is remarkably prolonged once labor is established and analgesics are administered.

459
00:54:52,604 --> 00:54:59,254
As a consequence, ingested food and most medications remain in the stomach and are poorly absorbed.

460
00:54:59,254 --> 00:55:02,104
They may be vomited and aspirated .

461
00:55:02,104 --> 00:55:16,816
However, oral intake of moderate amounts of clear liquids is reasonable for women with uncomplicated labor (American Academy of Pediatrics, 2017; American Society of Anesthesiologists, 2016).

462
00:55:16,816 --> 00:55:24,266
Water, clear tea, black coffee, carbonated beverages, Popsicles, and pulp-free juices are options.

463
00:55:24,266 --> 00:55:33,191
In those with appreciable risks for aspiration or those with significant risks for cesarean delivery, further restriction may be instituted.

464
00:55:33,191 --> 00:55:42,716
For example, for those with planned cesarean delivery, liquids are halted 2 hours before and solids are stopped 6 to 8 hours prior to surgery .

465
00:55:42,716 --> 00:55:52,341
Intravenous Fluids Although an IV infusion system is often routinely established early in labor, real need for this in the normal pregnant woman is limited.

466
00:55:52,341 --> 00:55:58,904
Access allows parenteral analgesia or IV fluid infusion prior to regional analgesia.

467
00:55:58,904 --> 00:56:08,416
In the immediate puerperium, a dilute oxytocin solution can be given prophylactically to prevent uterine atony and at times therapeutically to treat it.

468
00:56:08,416 --> 00:56:21,641
Moreover, with longer labors, the administration of glucose, sodium, and water to the otherwise fasting woman at the rate of 60 to 120 mL/hr prevents dehydration and acidosis.

469
00:56:21,641 --> 00:56:31,416
Although not mandatory, IV hydration may shorten labor length, and most studies of fluid selection use first-stage labor length as a primary outcome.

470
00:56:31,416 --> 00:56:40,879
From these investigations, data support use of fluids containing dextrose compared with fluid without it (Riegel, 2018; Shrivastava,

471
00:56:41,044 --> 00:56:55,544
2009). For administration, rates of 125 or 250 mL/hr are suitable (Dawood, 2013; Ehsanipoor, 2017; Fong, 2017).

472
00:56:55,494 --> 00:57:09,894
In our practice, saline with 5-percent dextrose is infused at a rate of 150 mL/hr. Rupture of Membranes If the membranes are intact, temptation is great, even during normal labor, to perform amniotomy.

473
00:57:09,894 --> 00:57:21,281
Benefits are earlier detection of meconium-stained amnionic fluid and the opportunity to apply an electrode to the fetus or insert a pressure catheter into the uterine cavity for monitoring.

474
00:57:21,281 --> 00:57:28,144
In one metaanalysis of 15 studies, normal spontaneous labor was not shortened by amniotomy .

475
00:57:28,144 --> 00:57:34,256
Risks include potential uterine infection if performed early and possible cord prolapse.

476
00:57:34,256 --> 00:57:42,244
To help prevent prolapse, the fetal head must be well applied to the cervix and not dislodged from the pelvis during amniotomy.

477
00:57:42,244 --> 00:57:49,569
The use of amniotomy in the setting of abnormal labor is discussed in Chapter 23 (p. 434).

478
00:57:49,569 --> 00:58:02,506
With prolonged membrane rupture and unknown group B streptococcal (GBS) status, antimicrobial administration for prevention of GBS infections is recommended for intrapartum risk factors .

479
00:58:02,506 --> 00:58:13,931
These include rupture of membranes greater than 18 hours or intrapartum temperature >38.0°C or >100.4°F .

480
00:58:13,931 --> 00:58:19,344
This practice similarly lowers rates of chorioamnionitis and endometritis .

481
00:58:19,344 --> 00:58:28,406
Urinary Bladder Function Distention of the bladder can hinder descent of the fetal presenting part and lead to subsequent bladder hypotonia and infection.

482
00:58:28,406 --> 00:58:33,631
Periodically, the suprapubic region is palpated to detect distention.

483
00:58:33,631 --> 00:58:40,169
If the bladder is readily seen or palpated above the symphysis, the woman should be encouraged to void.

484
00:58:40,169 --> 00:58:45,856
Those unable to do so on a bedpan may be able to ambulate with assistance to a toilet.

485
00:58:45,856 --> 00:58:51,356
Catheterization is indicated if the bladder is distended and voiding is not possible.

486
00:58:51,356 --> 00:58:54,706
Regional analgesia is a common reason.

487
00:58:54,706 --> 00:59:05,144
In these cases, continuous or intermittent catheterization is suitable, and both have comparable rates of puerperal urinary tract infection and urinary retention .

488
00:59:05,144 --> 00:59:10,769
Postpartum retention is discussed in Chapter 36 (p. 643).

489
00:59:10,769 --> 00:59:14,156
# Management of Second-stage Labor.

490
00:59:14,156 --> 00:59:20,319
In later first-stage or early second-stage labor, attempts are made to determine head position.

491
00:59:20,319 --> 00:59:27,044
The fingers are directed posteriorly and then swept forward over the fetal head toward the maternal symphysis .

492
00:59:27,044 --> 00:59:33,556
During this movement, the fingers necessarily cross the sagittal suture, and its lie is delineated.

493
00:59:33,556 --> 00:59:37,794
Next, the positions of the two fontanels are ascertained.

494
00:59:37,794 --> 00:59:46,981
For this, fingers are passed to the most anterior extension of the sagittal suture, and the fontanel encountered there is examined and identified.

495
00:59:46,981 --> 00:59:54,594
The fingers then pass along the sagittal suture to the other end of the head until the other fontanel is felt and differentiated .

496
00:59:54,594 --> 01:00:01,931
Last, the station, or extent to which the presenting part has descended into the pelvis, also can be established.

497
01:00:01,931 --> 01:00:07,069
Using these maneuvers, the various sutures and fontanels are determined.

498
01:00:07,069 --> 01:00:13,231
In unclear cases, sonography can be used as described earlier (p. 420).

499
01:00:13,231 --> 01:00:15,819
Figure 22-10.

500
01:00:15,819 --> 01:00:19,919
Locating the sagittal suture by vaginal examination.

501
01:00:19,919 --> 01:00:22,569
Figure 22-11.

502
01:00:22,569 --> 01:00:26,706
Differentiating the fontanels by vaginal examination.

503
01:00:26,706 --> 01:00:34,144
With full cervical dilation, which signifies the onset of the second stage, a woman typically begins to bear down.

504
01:00:34,144 --> 01:00:38,919
With descent of the presenting part, she develops the urge to defecate.

505
01:00:38,919 --> 01:00:47,119
Uterine contractions and the accompanying expulsive forces may now last 1 minute and recur at intervals less than 90 seconds.

506
01:00:47,119 --> 01:00:56,856
As discussed earlier, the median duration of the second stage is 50 minutes in nulliparas and 20 minutes in multiparas, although this length can vary.

507
01:00:56,856 --> 01:01:10,606
Monitoring intervals of the fetal heart rate were discussed earlier (p. 427), and interpretation of second-stage electronic fetal heart rate patterns is discussed in Chapter 24 (p. 447).

508
01:01:10,606 --> 01:01:16,456
In most cases, bearing down is reflexive and spontaneous during secondstage labor.

509
01:01:16,456 --> 01:01:21,144
In those with neuraxial analgesia, this urge may be blunted.

510
01:01:21,144 --> 01:01:31,606
To improve spontaneous birth rates, delayed pushing for 60 minutes once the second stage is reached has been suggested to permit passive fetal descent to increase pushing

511
01:01:31,756 --> 01:01:35,181
efficiency and minimize maternal exhaustion.

512
01:01:35,131 --> 01:01:42,493
One large randomized trial compared immediate and delayed pushing in nulliparas at term with neuraxial analgesia.

513
01:01:42,493 --> 01:01:45,118
Routes of delivery were similar.

514
01:01:45,118 --> 01:01:52,743
The immediate group actively pushed longer, a mean difference of 9 minutes, but had lower rates of chorioamnionitis .

515
01:01:52,743 --> 01:01:56,606
A subsequent metaanalysis noted similar findings .

516
01:01:56,606 --> 01:02:03,368
During pushing, a woman may not employ her expulsive forces to good advantage and coaching is desirable.

517
01:02:03,368 --> 01:02:10,743
When the next uterine contraction begins, she is instructed to exert downward pressure as though she were straining at stool.

518
01:02:10,743 --> 01:02:15,756
A woman is not encouraged to push beyond the completion of each contraction.

519
01:02:15,756 --> 01:02:20,081
Instead, she and her fetus are allowed to rest and recover.

520
01:02:20,081 --> 01:02:30,718
During this period of active pushing, the fetal heart rate auscultated during the contraction is likely to be slow but should recover to normal range before the next expulsive effort.

521
01:02:30,718 --> 01:02:41,856
Fetal and obstetrical outcomes appear to be unaffected whether pushing is coached or uncoached during second-stage labor (Bloom, 2006; Tuuli, 2012).

522
01:02:41,856 --> 01:02:47,568
Several positions during the second stage have been recommended to augment pushing efforts.

523
01:02:47,568 --> 01:02:58,168
The Birth in the Upright Maternal Position with Epidural in Second Stage (BUMPES) trial compared a lateral recumbent position and a supported upright one for nulliparas.

524
01:02:58,168 --> 01:03:03,731
Upright positions include standing, sitting out of bed, kneeling, or squatting.

525
01:03:03,731 --> 01:03:10,643
The lateral recumbent position required the head of the bed to have an incline ≤30 degrees.

526
01:03:10,643 --> 01:03:22,356
When compared, the recumbent group had a spontaneous birth rate of 41 percent, which was significantly higher than the 35-percent spontaneous birth rate experienced by the upright group.

527
01:03:22,356 --> 01:03:30,756
Otherwise, rates of operative vaginal delivery (OVD), anal sphincter tear, and neonatal outcome measures were similar .

528
01:03:30,756 --> 01:03:43,593
In a subsequent Cochrane review of studies that included nulliparas and multiparas, upright or recumbent position had little or no affect on rates of “operative birth,” which combined OVD and cesarean delivery .

529
01:03:43,593 --> 01:03:57,793
In those without epidural analgesia, Gupta and coworkers (2017) in their Cochrane review compared upright positions with supine or lithotomy positions during second-stage labor in multiparas and nulliparas.

530
01:03:57,793 --> 01:04:04,731
Upright positions offered a marginally shorter interval to delivery and fewer episiotomies and OVDs.

531
01:04:04,731 --> 01:04:12,331
However, rates of blood loss >500 mL and perhaps of second-degree lacerations were increased.

532
01:04:12,331 --> 01:04:16,131
In sum, no one position is mandated for labor.

533
01:04:16,131 --> 01:04:25,518
Repositioning with the goal of maternal comfort and optimal fetal trajectory is suitable as long as appropriate maternal and fetal monitoring can be achieved .

534
01:04:25,518 --> 01:04:36,243
To help avoid femoral or lumbosacral nerve injury from lithotomy positions, hips are not overly flexed, abducted, or externally rotated for extended periods .

535
01:04:36,243 --> 01:04:47,306
From early data, transperineal ultrasound assessment of the fetal head’s angle of progression relative to the symphysis during the second stage may be a tool to predict vaginal birth .

536
01:04:47,306 --> 01:04:54,168
As the head descends through the pelvis, the perineum begins to bulge and the overlying skin becomes stretched.

537
01:04:54,168 --> 01:04:58,493
Now the scalp of the fetus may be visible through the vulvar opening.

538
01:04:58,493 --> 01:05:06,731
At this time, the woman and her fetus are prepared for delivery, which is described in Chapter 27 (p. 498).

539
01:05:06,731 --> 01:05:09,293
Labor Management Protocols.

540
01:05:09,293 --> 01:05:17,231
An orderly and systematic approach to labor management results in reproducible beneficial maternal and perinatal outcomes .

541
01:05:17,231 --> 01:05:30,568
In Dublin more than 30 years ago, O’Driscoll and associates (1984) pioneered the concept that a disciplined, standardized labor management protocol reduced the number of cesarean deliveries for dystocia.

542
01:05:30,568 --> 01:05:37,956
Their overall cesarean delivery rate was 5 percent in the 1970s and 1980s with such management.

543
01:05:37,956 --> 01:05:42,143
The approach is now referred to as active management of labor.

544
01:05:42,143 --> 01:05:51,331
Two of its components—amniotomy and oxytocin—have been widely used, especially in English-speaking countries outside the United States.

545
01:05:51,331 --> 01:06:01,156
With this protocol, labor is diagnosed when painful contractions are accompanied by complete cervical effacement, bloody “show,” or ruptured membranes.

546
01:06:01,156 --> 01:06:02,843
Women with

547
01:06:03,004 --> 01:06:07,116
such findings are committed to delivery within 12 hours.

548
01:06:07,066 --> 01:06:14,204
Pelvic examination is performed each hour for the next 3 hours, and thereafter at 2hour intervals.

549
01:06:14,204 --> 01:06:20,616
When dilation has not increased by at least 1 cm/hr, amniotomy is performed.

550
01:06:20,616 --> 01:06:33,966
Progress is again assessed at 2 hours, and high- dose oxytocin infusion, described in Chapter 26 (p. 493), is started unless dilation of at least 1 cm/hr is attained.

551
01:06:33,966 --> 01:06:37,329
Women are constantly attended by midwives.

552
01:06:37,329 --> 01:06:43,779
If membranes rupture before admission, oxytocin is begun for no progress at the 1-hour mark.

553
01:06:43,779 --> 01:06:55,366
López-Zeno and colleagues (1992) prospectively compared such active management with their “traditional” approach to labor management at Northwestern Memorial Hospital in Chicago.

554
01:06:55,366 --> 01:07:02,691
They randomly assigned 705 nulliparas with uncomplicated pregnancies in spontaneous labor at term.

555
01:07:02,691 --> 01:07:12,604
The cesarean delivery rate was significantly lower with active versus traditional management—10.5 versus 14.1 percent, respectively.

556
01:07:12,604 --> 01:07:15,654
Subsequent studies did not show this.

557
01:07:15,654 --> 01:07:26,941
Wei and associates (2013) in a Cochrane database review found a modest reduction in cesarean delivery rates when active management of labor was compared with standard care.

558
01:07:26,941 --> 01:07:37,416
Frigoletto and coworkers (1995) reported another randomized trial with 1934 nulliparous women at Brigham and Women’s Hospital in Boston.

559
01:07:37,416 --> 01:07:44,116
Although they found that such management somewhat shortened labor, it did not affect the cesarean delivery rate.

560
01:07:44,116 --> 01:07:47,941
These observations have since been reported by others .

561
01:07:47,941 --> 01:07:54,916
At Parkland Hospital, women are admitted if active labor is diagnosed or if ruptured membranes are confirmed.

562
01:07:54,916 --> 01:08:03,016
Labor is defined as cervical dilation of ≥4 cm in the presence of regular uterine contractions.

563
01:08:03,016 --> 01:08:10,404
Management guidelines direct that a pelvic examination subsequently be performed approximately every 2 hours.

564
01:08:10,404 --> 01:08:17,054
Ineffective labor is suspected when the cervix does not dilate within approximately 2 hours of admission.

565
01:08:17,054 --> 01:08:23,391
Amniotomy is then performed, and labor progress determined at the next 2-hour evaluation.

566
01:08:23,391 --> 01:08:30,604
In women whose labors do not progress, an intrauterine pressure catheter is placed to assess uterine function.

567
01:08:30,604 --> 01:08:43,866
Hypotonic contractions and no cervical dilation after an additional 2 to 3 hours result in stimulation of labor using the high-dose oxytocin regimen described in Chapter 26 (p. 493).

568
01:08:43,866 --> 01:08:51,854
The goal is uterine activity of 200 to 250 Montevideo units for 4 hours before dystocia is diagnosed.

569
01:08:51,854 --> 01:09:03,566
In cases in which hypotonic contractions are strongly suspected, internal monitors may be placed with amniotomy and again cervical change and contraction pattern are assessed in 2 hours.

570
01:09:03,566 --> 01:09:11,841
Confirmation of deficient Montevideo units at that time may prompt oxytocin augmentation for maternal or fetal indications.

571
01:09:11,841 --> 01:09:21,716
Dilation rates of 1 to 2 cm/hr are accepted as evidence of progress after satisfactory uterine activity has been established with oxytocin.

572
01:09:21,716 --> 01:09:28,016
This can require up to 8 hours or more before cesarean delivery is performed for dystocia.

573
01:09:28,016 --> 01:09:35,704
The cumulative time required to effect this stepwise management approach permits many women to establish effective labor.

574
01:09:35,704 --> 01:09:42,816
This management protocol has been evaluated in >20,000 women with uncomplicated pregnancies.

575
01:09:42,816 --> 01:09:51,241
Importantly, these labor interventions and the relatively infrequent use of cesarean delivery did not jeopardize the fetus-newborn.

